<h2>The fare decides, and it decides when you buy</h2>
<p>
    Tickets are nonrefundable and nontransferable unless the fare says
    otherwise. Every result and every checkout says which yours is, on two
    lines &mdash; <strong>changes</strong> and <strong>cancellation</strong>
    &mdash; each marked free, for a fee, or not allowed.
</p>
<p>
    Those two are separate. A fare can let you cancel and still return no
    money: cancelling gives up the seat, a refund gives back the fare, and only
    a refundable fare does both.
</p>

<h2>On a connection, the tightest flight decides</h2>
<p>
    A journey is only as flexible as its strictest leg. A fare that forbids
    changes on the second flight forbids them for the whole journey, whatever
    the first flight allowed.
</p>

<h2>Cancelling a booking</h2>
<p>
    Open <a href="/my/bookings">My bookings</a> and cancel it from the card for
    that trip. You will be asked to confirm first. It then moves to
    <a href="/my/bookings/cancelled">cancelled bookings</a> and keeps a page of
    its own, so you can still send it to someone &mdash; but the flights are no
    longer held and the reference will not check in.
</p>

<h2>Changing dates</h2>
<p>
    Not something this site does. Where the fare allows changes, ask the
    airline: it sets the fee and charges any difference in fare. Where it does
    not, the only way round is to cancel, if the fare lets you, and book again.
</p>

<h2>Getting money back</h2>
<p>
    Whatever the fare allows is settled by the airline, so quote your booking
    reference to it. The booking&rsquo;s own page holds the contact details
    given at checkout, and the airline&rsquo;s page here holds its phone
    number.
</p>
